With regards to your message at 09:58 AM 6/12/01, Christopher C. Chimelis. 
Where you stated:

>On Tue, 12 Jun 2001, Maurice Hilarius wrote:
>
> > Red Hat and SuSE dominate in the commercial distribution market for Alpha.
> > As such they are good starting points, but it is never a bad thing to see
> > what the major updates are for this, as with any platform.
> > There are quite a few other smaller and derivative distributions, ranging
> > from Debian to other commercial endeavors.
>
>I'm not going to start a flamewar here, although it would be easy for me
>to do so :-)  To answer the question more directly, RedHat does have an
>Alpha distribution, as do SuSE, Debian, and Slackware.  My advice: use the
>distribution that you're most familiar with.

I concur.
People often forget it IS Linux, and that distributions are just 
"snapshots" of what a company decided to package at some given point in 
time. As progress continues, and nothing stops when a distro is made, it is 
best to consider these as simply "starting points".
Naturally if you are a fan of "Distribution X", stick to that, as it is 
going to give you the shortest learning curve to get comfortable.


> > >2. How compatible are 7300's? I'd especially like to hear from someone 
> who's
> > >already installed Linux on a 7300 (4100 with DEC marketroid 
> braindamage, as
> > >Paul from COLA put it).
> >
> > Many people have successfully installed on that platform.
>
>This is very true.  The Rawhide family is supported by the kernel and has
>been for some time.  BTW, I like the "marketroid braindamage" snippet
>above:-P

Be nice. I am NOT a "marketroid".
We build and sell Alpha computers.

We do our own distro, called Northern Linux, which is based on Red Hat
We feel that more timely updates are needed for our customers who buy 
machines and support from us, so we add to, and update the Red Hat version.

I CAN say that Red Hat has the most history in the Alpha Linux market. That 
is not an endorsement that they are best, however, it is what we have 
chosen to start with for several years so far..


