Crucial is a good site for memory, but the part
numbers they give are very out of date and you'll be
hard pressed to use the memory elsewhere (migration,
testing,etc).  The part numbers provided are for PC66
SDRAM, not PC100 parts.  This works because Pyxis
(21174) is a 66MHz part.  10ns (83.5MHz) SDRAM (the
unofficial PC83 spec) is similiarly limited to older
"pre 440BX" chipset Intel machines.

Buy more "common" 168 pin "PC100" unbuffered ECC SDRAM
with a CAS2 latency.  This is the same memory used in
Intel 440BX based systems. You'll not only pay less,
you'll get a newer, more volume part number.  It will
run at the lower clock of 66MHz on the Alpha EV56 with
an even higher margin than the PC66 would (its a
higher grade component tested good at a higher
frequency). Most of the 128MB modules of this type are
single sided and I believe single banked. Can anyone
confirm internal banking? Pyxis will use either,
though I think the dual banked may be slower if my own
biological memory serves.

--KE





