> > Look, I'm just pointing out that it's a stable situation. If you ask
> > inflammatory questions, what good do you think it will come out of it?
> 
> I don't think anything will come of it, frankly.

So then why post?

> Apparently, the only
> people who "matter" like the status quo, so why even entertain my rant?

No, I think it's clear that people don't see any _constructive_ action
to be taken.

> For the record, "stable" doesn't always equal "best situation or
> compromise".

Of course not.

> What history shows that we're often "in synch"?  Take a look back at the
> state of Alpha even as far back as 2.0.x.  What history shows is that it
> frequently takes more than two minor revisions of the kernel to get Alpha
> "in sync" with the changes made to the rest of the code.

I call that "often in synch". A real fork is one in which you don't
closely track the other fork. And when Jay was maintaining Alpha
patches, he was closely tracking the main kernel, just like Alan
closely tracks Linus now.

> Apparently, though, you either misunderstand my overall point with what I
> wrote.  I was always taught that the only dumb questions were the ones not
> asked, so I think that asking repeatedly is sometimes worthwhile since it
> just might change a not-so-good situation.

Or you could cause a lot of flammage, and no constructive action.

> In short, this discussion may be a "kernel politics" hot-plate, but
> I don't think that taking a "let's not rock the boat or upset
> anyone" approach is working in the best interest of the development of the
> Alpha portions of the kernel.

Apparently you have mistaken my writings with a "let's not rock the
boat" attitude. I'd suggest re-reading what I've written. What I _am_
saying is that this isn't the place to discuss overall kernel
politics, and that this port has had adequate success in the past
keeping up with the main kernel.

-- g




