Content-Type: text/plain; charset="iso-8859-1"; format=flowed
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable

I think the main thing to consider in all of this is that Compaq has a need=
=20
to continue to support TRU64 and VMS on their hardware. It will certainly=20
take some time to port the OS to Itanium, and it will also take time for=20
Itanium to achieve anything resembling Alpha performance, both for existing=
=20
and future generations.

Here is the quote from the Compaq press release that is both relevant, and=
=20
gives a bit of an idea about what lies ahead:

"Compaq will immediately begin to port Tru64 UNIX, OpenVMS and NonStop=20
Kernel operating systems and development tools to the Itanium processor=20
family. Operating system and application development tools compatibility=20
protects customers' long-term investments in Tru64 UNIX, OpenVMS, and=20
NonStop Kernel, as well as advancing the capabilities for Windows 2000 and=
=20
Linux on ProLiant."

In other words at least a year, probably 2 or 3 to move the OS over.


"Technology Transfer
Under the multi-year technology agreement, Compaq is transferring=20
significant Alpha tools and engineering resources to Intel, as well as=20
granting licenses to Compaq's Alpha microprocessor technology and compilers.=
 "

Intel now has access to the technologies and people who can make Itanium=20
faster.


"AlphaServer, NonStop, ProLiant Roadmaps
Compaq said it will continue to design and build new AlphaServer=99 systems=
=20
based on current and upcoming Alpha processor technology through 2003.  The=
=20
company plans to upgrade the current high-end AlphaServer GS Series with a=
=20
1 GHz Alpha processor this summer. The next-generation EV7 Alpha processor,=
=20
which is currently under development, will power a new AlphaServer system=20
planned for introduction late next year. "

In other words it will take at least 2 more years to phase out Alpha.

Yes, this is the end of Alpha, but not for at least a couple of years.

Really I think this will be very good for Alpha products to migrate in to=20
the "low cost market" that people have been asking for.
With the existing cost investment in Alpha, I think Compaq will want to=20
squeeze every penny it can on what it has already paid for..

And for us Linux users the good news is that we will finally reach a point=
=20
where we don't have the endless situation where the updates to kernel and=20
libs lag between x86 and Alpha..
