With regards to your message at 01:19 PM 6/25/01, Michal Jaegermann. Where 
you stated:
>On Mon, Jun 25, 2001 at 10:39:44AM -0600, Maurice Hilarius wrote:
> > It is official:
> > http://www.compaq.com/hps/ipf-enterprise/index.html
>...
> > Once again Compaq has shown a typical lack of long term planning.
>
>Reading between lines this also smells to me like an admission from
>Intel that they have big troubles with Itanic.  Especially that time
>scales mentioned in all these annoucements sound like ethernity in
>this business.  More so if you remember Intel pronoucements from,
>when it was - four or five years ago, that Merced (it was called that
>way at that time, right?) is "right behind the corner".  If everything
>will go as planned (a very big IF) I would would be not very
>surprised to find out that future ia-64 variants look awfully close
>to a rebadged Alpha. :-)  But in these years ahead they are talking
>about we can still see quite a few other sharp turns.  Enjoy the ride!

Absolutely. But thanks to Compaq they got everything they wanted:
Alpha technology to make Itanium work.
Compaq to abandon Alpha in the long run. Even though it is clear it will 
take at least 2 years, and more likely 4 to do this, it effectively kills 
Alpha sales from now on.


>Compaq is quite interesting with "we are not making anymore but
>we are still making for a while" on the referenced page.  Still the
>biggest kick one can get from "read what our customers are saying"
>in that place. :-)

Yeah, and that is a big factor. a LOT of companies they supply want them to 
do only X86, as it is cheaper, and more "mainstream".
Frankly Michal, this was inevitable.
The bitch of it is that Samsung now loses their biggest customer for Alpha CPUs
Are they going to invest in making EV7 or later? I doubt it.

Which will cut us out of the Alpha market.. how soon I don't know.

