If you can't tell from the subject, it's HOLY WAR time!

Someone here suggested an open-source processor.  I hear that there's
already a SPARC-compatable processor under LGPL.  If we're going to do
something like that, however, we ought to do it right.

The LISPM.  Fifteen years ahead of its time then, fifteen years ahead
of its time now.

Now, I'm not only suggesting this because of some religious beliefs, but
also because

1.) There's a DEC Alpha simulation of the old LISPM OS Genera floating
around out there:
<http://stony-brook.scrc.symbolics.com/www/next-release.html>
2.) The LISPM developers might be interested in developing for this,
again.
3.) DEC Alpha developers might be interested in something superior, and
non-Intel.
4.) (as I undertand it) The instruction set is simple.  Creating a chip
based on that set will be about as simple as making a processor for
anything.

If we were to get even a low-quality, slow prototype, we might be able
to attract others.

Here's what I envision:

- A WMU like the VAX had.  Well, actually, like the Transmeta Crusoe
  has.
- A LISP primitive based instruction set
- 256 bit ibstuction set (yes, you heard right!  You'd use the most
  efficient set of primitives)
- All those other goodies that LISPMS have (hardware support for
  garbage collection, type checking, &c.)

Whaddya think?

-- 
